# Applied before assembly: exemplar quantities are pulled only from source text.
E={
'pytorch_input_tensors':'Convert each array with torch.from_numpy and cast to its separately declared dtype. Inspect shape, dtype and observation order, then assert axes and common batch length before returning the pair. Conversion can succeed while alignment fails: the supplied three-target case must be rejected by assertions. Test the accepted pair, boundary and incompatible contracts separately. A target-representation failure is not a reason to change the feature dtype.',
'dataset_minibatch_loader':'The same index must retrieve both parts of __getitem__’s tuple. Assert __len__, item identities and observed batch boundaries. Retaining versus dropping the incomplete batch changes the consumed samples; it does not manufacture a full batch. Check pairings under the declared shuffle policy instead of treating output position as source-row identity. Well-shaped batches can still be wrongly paired if indexing was wrong.',
'neural_model_definition':'Trace each per-observation width, then preserve the leading batch axis throughout. The intermediate Linear output feeds elementwise ReLU and then the final Linear. Register layers in an nn.Module with forward, or use the declared Sequential composition. Verify output shape and the task’s final activation requirement. Changing batch size cannot repair adjacent feature-width incompatibility.',
'autograd_gradients':'Compute the weight reference as X.T @ D and the bias reference as D.sum(axis=0), without dividing again: D includes reduction. Set trainable leaves to require gradients and form the scalar loss. Clear old gradients before backward, then inspect .grad before clearing it. Assert reference shapes and values with explicit relative and absolute tolerances, including a deliberately mismatched failure case.',
'task_loss_selection':'Keep the three loss contracts distinct. MSE averages squared differences and requires matching shapes. The binary-logit derivative uses sigmoid(logit) minus target, divided by output-element count; it is not a probability-input loss contract. Class-index cross-entropy takes raw logits and long target indices with the declared class width, using softmax-minus-one-hot divided by sample count. Check reduction and representation before interpreting scalar or gradient evidence.',
'optimizer_configuration':'Retain the pre-step parameter and current gradient, then compute the conditional reference before comparing the post-step value under tolerance. Initialize SGD with model.parameters(); zero old gradients, backpropagate and step. Backward without step gives gradient evidence but not this update. Keep accepted, boundary and failure assertions. This equation is for plain SGD without momentum, weight decay or other modifications.',
'minibatch_training_loop':'For each paired minibatch, compute predictions and loss, zero old gradients, backpropagate and step. Store loss.item() with batch count rather than interpreting every batch as full. In the deterministic plain-SGD case, retain before/after parameters and assert the observed change equals −learning_rate × current_gradient under tolerance. Test accepted, boundary and failure cases so leftover gradients or a missing step cannot masquerade as the intended loop.',
 'training_evaluation_modes':'Assert permitted training masking/scaling, not an arbitrary random mask. Evaluation-mode dropout returns its input; training-mode dropout can still mask under no_grad. Validation needs eval plus no_grad and no optimizer step. Restore train before the next training pass. Check accepted, boundary and failure predicates so layer behavior, graph recording and parameter updates remain separate observations.',
 'weight_persistence':'Reload with weights_only=True into the same compatible architecture and set eval. Keep the original evaluation reference, then compare restored predictions on the identical fixture under explicit tolerances. Compare corresponding state_dict entries too: a coincidental matching prediction is not a complete parameter-state check. Use binary write/read handles and assertion fixtures that distinguish incompatible architecture or differing entries from successful restoration.',
 'validation_early_stopping':'Trace current value, accepted best and counter separately. Accept only current < best − min_delta; otherwise increment the consecutive non-improvement count and break when patience is exhausted. Retain the most recent qualifying checkpoint, not automatically the lowest observation or last weights. Deep-copy or save its state before later updates. Bound the while loop by maximum epochs as well, and exclude protected test observations from preprocessing fitting, selection and tuning.',
 'training_failure_diagnosis':'Read .grad after backward and before clearing it, then trace loss through outputs to the selected parameter. Check target representation, loss computation and declared learning rate. Test one supported root-cause correction on the identical fixture. Compare loss and derivative signs, magnitudes and assertion records against the failing reference. A different task or several simultaneous changes would not isolate this hypothesis.',
 'device_placement':'Inspect authorized VM identity/status, start it, wait and connect only within approved scope. Check CUDA availability and select the supported CUDA or CPU path. Move model, features and targets; inspect their devices and verify a bounded forward/backward fixture with assertions. Moving features alone leaves a possible target mismatch. Stop after the workload, wait and verify the resulting stopped state.',
 'training_batch_sizing':'Add feature and target element-byte estimates, but do not label that sum total training memory. Change only DataLoader batch size with task and alignment fixed. Reset CUDA peak statistics before each bounded trial and inspect peak allocated tensor bytes afterward. Match the budget to the memory quantity it governs. Among eligible trials, compare completed work per elapsed time; higher throughput cannot rescue a memory failure.',
 'training_memory_diagnosis':'Add bias, gradient, activation and batch allocations using their own element widths and simultaneous lifetimes. Compare allocated and reserved peaks at the failure point. If saved diagnostics unnecessarily retain graphs, use detached/scalar values. Change one supported factor, rerun the same task, and verify completion and measured peak. empty_cache cannot release live tensor storage.',
 'training_bottleneck_profiling':'If loading dominates the attributed work, test one supported loading factor, not an unrelated model change. Use same-window CPU, memory and process evidence, including docker stats and docker top for the container fixture. Compare the same workload without profiler overhead after the change. On CUDA, synchronize timing boundaries to include completed work. Attribution and final unprofiled timing remain separate checks.',
 'training_hardware_selection':'Compare both options against the same task-quality and memory limits, including relevant movement and warm-up interpretation. Calculate execution cost from measured duration and the supplied rate rather than speed or utilization alone. Inspect identity and status; start/connect only to authorized instances. After both benchmarks, stop both and verify states. Retain measured lifecycle durations for billing interpretation; disk and other charges may remain.',
 'task_specific_pretrained_weight_fine_tuning':'Keep the original mapping for the frozen reference and use the checkpoint’s processor for both image cohorts. Freeze existing parameters, construct the task head from hidden width, and update both label maps and num_labels. Verify logits/long-target contracts, selected head gradient agreement and changed head with unchanged backbone. Compare matched validation predictions using conditional FPR/FNR and supplied deployment cost probabilities; empty conditioning groups have undefined rates. Protected test observations cannot fit preprocessing, choose models or tune settings.'}
